T06.01|Current versus non-current classification communicates the expected cycle of asset realisation and liability settlement. It is not merely a permanent label attached to an account name. Operating-cycle items can be current even when their cycle exceeds twelve months. Other criteria include trading purpose, expected timing and the right to defer settlement; the exact liability rules depend on the applicable framework and period.~~Classification affects liquidity ratios but does not itself create liquidity. A current asset can be difficult to sell or collect, while a long-term obligation can cause near-term stress through interest or covenants. Read maturity and restriction disclosures alongside the face of the balance sheet.|Operating-cycle items: Inventory, trade receivables and trade payables arising within the normal operating cycle.;;Other current items: Assets or obligations meeting relevant near-term, trading or other current-classification criteria.;;Non-current items: Resources and obligations outside current classification.;;Current maturity of long-term debt: The portion due in the near term rather than the original loan's full balance.|A firm has ₹100 lakh of five-year debt, with ₹20 contractually due next year and ₹80 later. Assume no covenant breach or other classification change.~~Separate the scheduled ₹20 near-term repayment.;;Present ₹20 as current and ₹80 as non-current under the simplified maturity assumption.;;Compare the ₹20 due with available cash, collections and committed funding.~~A loan originally issued for five years can contain both current and non-current portions.~~A refinancing intention is not identical to an enforceable right to defer settlement. Assess the actual terms and applicable reporting rules.|Identify the normal operating cycle.;;Read maturity schedules, restrictions and covenants.;;Assess usability and cash timing separately from current labels.
T06.02|Cash includes immediately available cash balances; cash equivalents are qualifying short-term, highly liquid investments convertible to known cash amounts with insignificant value-change risk. Under IAS 7 they are held to meet short-term cash commitments rather than ordinary investment purposes, and normally have a short maturity from acquisition.~~A quoted fund, long-term deposit or volatile security is not automatically a cash equivalent because it can be sold. Examine access restrictions, original maturity, currency and settlement risk. Cash-flow reconciliation also needs a consistent definition of the included cash balances.|Cash on hand and demand deposits: Immediately available cash resources, subject to the relevant access facts.;;Qualifying cash equivalents: Short-term liquidity instruments meeting the governing criteria.;;Restricted balances: Cash whose use is constrained and requires separate analysis.;;Other investments: Financial assets that may be liquid but do not meet cash-equivalent classification.|A company has ₹5 lakh physical and demand-account cash, a ₹10 qualifying short-term treasury instrument, and ₹8 equity shares. Assume the treasury instrument meets all cash-equivalent criteria.~~Include ₹5 cash and ₹10 qualifying equivalent in the selected cash measure.;;Keep the ₹8 shares as an investment because saleability does not remove price risk.;;Review any restrictions before treating the included ₹15 as available for general payments.~~Cash and equivalents total ₹15 under these assumptions, while broader financial assets total ₹23.~~Liquidity analysis can include other investments separately, using realistic sale timing, volatility and access constraints.|Read the cash-equivalent policy.;;Check original maturity, value risk and purpose.;;Reconcile cash-flow and balance-sheet definitions and disclose restrictions.
T06.03|Restricted cash is money subject to contractual, legal or operational limitations on use. Examples include pledged deposits, escrow balances and debt-service reserves. A company can own the balance while being unable to spend it on wages or dividends. Liquid investments are separately assessed for saleability, value risk and timing.~~A valuation often adds eligible excess cash outside operating value, but required operating cash and inaccessible balances need different treatment. Do not deduct restricted money from debt automatically or assume every deposit is distributable. Classification as current also does not guarantee unrestricted availability.|Pledged deposit: A balance securing an obligation.;;Escrow: Money held for specified contractual purposes or release conditions.;;Debt-service reserve: Cash dedicated to scheduled debt obligations.;;Liquid investment: An asset that can be monetised, with price and settlement risks evaluated separately.|Reported cash balances are ₹30 lakh, including ₹8 pledged deposit and ₹7 project reserve. Assume the other ₹15 is unrestricted and the restrictions prevent general use.~~Identify ₹8 + ₹7 = ₹15 constrained cash.;;General-purpose cash availability = ₹30 − ₹15 = ₹15.;;Retain the restricted balances as recognised assets while modelling their allowed uses separately.~~Reported cash of ₹30 does not mean ₹30 can fund an unexpected general operating payment.~~Restrictions can protect creditors yet reduce financial flexibility. Release timing and associated obligations determine the economic value of constrained funds.|List each restriction and release condition.;;Match restricted balances to associated claims.;;Separate unrestricted, operating-required and potentially excess cash.
T06.04|Trade receivables arise when recognised customer sales have not yet been collected. They are claims on customers rather than cash itself. Collection timing, disputes, concentration and credit deterioration affect both cash conversion and recoverability. An expected-credit-loss allowance reduces the recognised net asset under the applicable approach.~~A receivable roll-forward explains how opening claims, new credit sales, collections, write-offs and other changes produce the closing amount. Loss allowances and write-offs are different from collections: they adjust accounting claims without receiving customer money. Analyse receivable growth alongside sales and payment terms.|Gross receivables: Customer claims before the relevant loss allowance.;;Loss allowance: An estimate of eligible expected credit shortfalls.;;Past-due or disputed balances: Amounts needing focused collection and recoverability assessment.;;Write-off: Removal of a balance when the governing conditions are met, rather than a customer payment.|Opening gross receivables are ₹20 lakh. New credit sales are ₹50, collections ₹45 and write-offs ₹2. Assume closing loss allowance ₹3 and no other changes.~~Closing gross receivables = ₹20 + ₹50 − ₹45 − ₹2 = ₹23.;;Closing net receivables = ₹23 − ₹3 = ₹20.;;Customer cash collection remains ₹45; neither the write-off nor the allowance produces cash.~~Net receivables of ₹20 can hide changes in gross exposure and estimated losses.~~Inspect both the movement schedule and ageing. A stable net figure can result from rising unpaid sales offset by larger loss allowances.|Reconcile gross and net balances.;;Review ageing, disputes, concentration and subsequent collections.;;Connect credit sales, allowance expense and working-capital cash effects.
T06.05|Inventory stores costs associated with goods not yet expensed through sale. Raw materials, incomplete production and completed goods represent different stages of the operating cycle. Inventory measurement also requires recoverability assessment; under IAS 2 the relevant comparison is cost versus net realisable value.~~Cost formulas such as FIFO and weighted average determine which eligible cost reaches expense when goods are sold. They can produce different margins and closing balances when purchase prices change. Inventory days and physical ageing are useful, but accounting value, physical quantity and commercial usefulness must be analysed separately.|Raw materials: Inputs awaiting consumption in production.;;Work in progress: Incomplete production with costs accumulated to date.;;Finished goods: Completed products awaiting sale.;;FIFO and weighted average: Cost-flow methods permitted under IAS 2; LIFO treatment differs under US GAAP.;;Write-down: Reduction when relevant net realisable value is below cost.|A retailer buys 100 units at ₹10 and 100 units at ₹12, then sells 150 units for ₹18 each. Assume no other costs.~~FIFO cost of sales = 100 × ₹10 + 50 × ₹12 = ₹1,600; closing inventory is ₹600.;;Weighted-average unit cost = ₹2,200/200 = ₹11; cost of sales is ₹1,650 and closing inventory ₹550.;;Revenue is ₹2,700 under either method.~~Gross profit is ₹1,100 under FIFO versus ₹1,050 under weighted average.~~Both methods allocate the same ₹2,200 purchase cost across expense and remaining inventory. A policy difference changes period comparisons without changing the purchases' total cash cost.|Trace quantity and cost movements separately.;;Review cost method, ageing and net realisable value.;;Connect stock growth with cash funding and future sales risk.
T06.06|A prepayment is payment for a benefit that will be consumed later, while an advance can be payment toward goods or services not yet received. These are assets only while the entity retains an eligible future benefit or recovery claim. They are not all interchangeable with customer receivables.~~The asset becomes expense or another asset as performance occurs. If the supplier fails or the benefit expires, recoverability may deteriorate. An increase can consume cash without immediately reducing profit, making prepayments important in operating cash-flow analysis.|Prepaid expense: Payment for a service or benefit recognised over future consumption periods.;;Supplier advance: Payment ahead of delivery, later transferred to the relevant purchase or recovered.;;Recoverable deposit: A claim refundable under specified contractual conditions.;;Other current asset: A presentation category requiring note-level analysis of its actual contents.|A business pays ₹12 lakh on 1 January for twelve months of insurance. Assume evenly consumed coverage, no refunds and no tax effects.~~Record ₹12 cash outflow and ₹12 prepaid asset on payment.;;Recognise ₹1 expense each month.;;At 31 March, accumulated expense is ₹3 and prepaid balance ₹9.~~Cash paid is ₹12, current expense is ₹3 and remaining recognised benefit is ₹9.~~Paying earlier shifts cash timing without changing the full-year ₹12 cost. The unexpired benefit supports the asset; a cancelled or invalid policy would require a different assessment.|Read the terms and remaining benefit period.;;Reconcile payment, consumption and recoverability.;;Distinguish operating prepayments from capital-asset advances and refundable deposits.
T06.07|Property, plant and equipment comprises tangible assets used for production, supply, administration or other qualifying continuing use. Initial eligible cost, subsequent additions, depreciation, impairment and disposal together explain the carrying amount. Gross cost is the accumulated eligible asset cost; net carrying amount deducts relevant depreciation and impairment.~~Cash spent on equipment and current depreciation are different events. A low net PPE balance may reflect old assets rather than low capacity, while a large new balance may not yet generate revenue. Review utilisation, component lives, maintenance needs and future replacement spending.|Gross PPE cost: Recorded eligible acquisition and subsequent capitalised costs.;;Accumulated depreciation: Cost allocation recognised over elapsed use.;;Impairment: A separate reduction for unsupported carrying value.;;Maintenance and expansion capex: Economic categories useful for forecasting, even when not separately disclosed in accounting.|Opening net PPE is ₹100 lakh. Qualifying additions are ₹20, depreciation ₹8, and equipment with carrying amount ₹5 is sold for ₹6. Assume no impairment or other movements.~~Closing net PPE = ₹100 + ₹20 − ₹8 − ₹5 = ₹107.;;Disposal gain = ₹6 − ₹5 = ₹1.;;Investing cash for these transactions is ₹6 − ₹20 = −₹14 under the assumed classification.~~PPE rises ₹7 while net cash investment is ₹14 and depreciation expense is ₹8.~~The difference is explained by allocation and disposal carrying value. Neither net PPE growth nor depreciation alone measures actual capex.|Use the PPE movement note.;;Distinguish gross, accumulated and net amounts.;;Link additions, utilisation and asset age to future cash generation.
T06.08|Capital work in progress records qualifying costs of assets being constructed or prepared for intended use. It is not the same as usable operating capacity. When an asset becomes available for intended use, it moves into the relevant asset category and depreciation ordinarily begins under the governing rules.~~Large CWIP balances can indicate expansion, long construction periods, delays or stalled projects. Costs may include eligible directly attributable expenditure and qualifying borrowing costs, but a project label does not justify every cost's capitalisation. Assess commissioning schedules, remaining cash commitments and recoverability.|Active construction: Assets progressing toward completion.;;Stalled or delayed projects: Costs needing evaluation of completion prospects and recoverability.;;Capital advances: Payments to suppliers that may be separately classified before eligible asset cost recognition.;;Commissioned assets: Completed assets available for intended use and transferred to operating categories.|Opening CWIP is ₹10 lakh and qualifying new construction spending is ₹30. A project costing ₹25 becomes available for use during the year. Assume no impairment.~~CWIP before transfer = ₹10 + ₹30 = ₹40.;;Transfer ₹25 to the appropriate PPE category.;;Closing CWIP = ₹15; begin depreciation on the commissioned asset from its eligible availability date.~~The ₹25 transfer changes asset categories without creating a new cash payment.~~Construction spending consumed cash earlier. Analysts must avoid counting the transfer as another capex outflow or assuming all CWIP already earns revenue.|Reconcile additions and commissioning transfers.;;Check ageing, delays, cost overruns and remaining commitments.;;Identify when depreciation and operating returns should start.
T06.09|A right-of-use asset represents a lessee's recognised right to use an underlying leased asset for a period. It is different from owning the physical asset outright. Under IFRS 16, recognition generally accompanies a lease liability, subject to the standard's scope and exemptions. Measurement can also reflect prepayments, incentives, direct costs and other eligible adjustments.~~Subsequent depreciation of the right-of-use asset and interest on the liability can change profit subtotals relative to a simple rental-expense view. These accounting changes do not alone improve operating economics. Compare companies using consistent lease treatment in cash flows and valuation claims.|Right-of-use asset: The recognised usage right held by a lessee.;;Lease liability: The recognised obligation for qualifying future lease payments.;;Leasehold improvements: Separate investments that may be PPE rather than the usage right itself.;;Exempt arrangements: Qualifying exemptions or out-of-scope contracts requiring specific assessment.|Assume an initial right-of-use asset and liability of ₹100 lakh, annual payment ₹25, first-year interest ₹8 and straight-line depreciation ₹20. Ignore all other adjustments.~~Recognise depreciation ₹20 on the asset.;;Apply ₹8 of the payment to interest and ₹17 to liability principal.;;Closing asset is ₹80 and liability ₹83 under these simplified assumptions.~~Asset and liability balances diverge because depreciation and principal repayment follow different schedules.~~Profit charges total ₹28 in this example, while cash paid is ₹25. EBITDA may exclude depreciation and interest but lease cash obligations continue.|Read lease terms, discount rates and maturity notes.;;Reconcile usage assets and lease liabilities separately.;;Use consistent lease treatment in earnings, cash flow and EV adjustments.
T06.10|An intangible asset is an eligible identifiable non-monetary resource without physical substance, such as acquired software, patents or contractual customer relationships. Goodwill is different: it is the residual recognised in a qualifying acquisition after identifiable net assets and other required components are assessed. A valuable reputation is not automatically recognised goodwill.~~Finite-lived intangibles are amortised over their useful lives; indefinite-lived classifications and goodwill follow applicable impairment requirements. Accounting carrying value can differ markedly from business value because internally generated advantages may not qualify for recognition.|Identifiable acquired intangible: A resource meeting separation or contractual/legal identification and recognition requirements.;;Qualifying development asset: Eligible internally developed expenditure recognised under the applicable framework.;;Goodwill: Acquisition-related residual rather than a separately identifiable operating resource.;;Finite versus indefinite life: A measurement and subsequent-accounting distinction requiring justified expectations.|A buyer pays ₹100 lakh for a business. Assume the fair value of identifiable net assets, including an acquired patent, is ₹80 and no other acquisition components affect the calculation.~~Identify and measure eligible assets and liabilities before residual calculation.;;Simplified goodwill = ₹100 − ₹80 = ₹20.;;If the patent is finite-lived, amortise it under its useful-life assumptions; assess goodwill under its impairment requirements.~~Goodwill is ₹20 under the stated simplified acquisition assumptions.~~The residual can reflect expected synergies, assembled capabilities or overpayment. Its recognition is not evidence that the acquisition will create ₹20 of realised value.|Separate identifiable intangibles and acquisition residuals.;;Review useful lives, amortisation and impairment assumptions.;;Compare carrying amounts with actual economic benefits and acquisition performance.
T06.11|Financial investments represent contractual financial claims, while investment property is qualifying property held for rent, capital appreciation or both rather than ordinary owner-occupied use. Their measurement and income recognition differ. Business purpose, contractual rights and the governing standards determine classification.~~A balance called investments can contain debt securities, listed shares, subsidiary holdings or associates with very different reporting treatment. An operating valuation should identify which assets already contribute to forecast cash flows and which are valued separately. Adding every investment balance to EV can double count economic benefits or substitute book amounts for value.|Debt investment: A contractual lending claim whose classification and measurement depend on applicable criteria.;;Equity investment: An ownership claim with relevant fair-value or other accounting treatment.;;Associate or subsidiary holding: An ownership relationship that may require equity method, consolidation or separate-account treatment.;;Investment property: Qualifying real estate held for rental income or appreciation.|A company owns an operating factory with carrying value ₹50 lakh, a rental building worth ₹30 and shares worth ₹10. Assume the factory's benefits are included in operating DCF, while rent and share returns are excluded.~~Keep the factory inside the operating valuation perimeter.;;Value the excluded rental property and shares separately using suitable methods.;;Add the eligible ₹30 + ₹10 once, after considering taxes, restrictions and other relevant adjustments.~~Separately assessed non-operating value is ₹40 under these simplified assumptions.~~If rental cash flows were already forecast in DCF, adding the full property value again would double count that asset.|Read the nature, ownership and measurement of each holding.;;Define operating versus non-operating scope.;;Avoid double counting income and asset value.
T06.12|Deferred tax reflects qualifying future tax consequences of differences between accounting carrying amounts and tax bases, plus relevant permitted items such as loss carryforwards. It is not simply unpaid current tax. A deferred tax asset requires the applicable recognition and recoverability assessment; a deferred tax liability represents a recognised future consequence under the governing rules.~~Temporary differences reverse over time, whereas permanent differences do not create the same deferred-tax mechanism. Tax rates and recognition rules are jurisdiction- and period-specific. The following numbers are hypothetical and teach the relationship rather than a current filing entitlement.|Deferred tax liability: A recognised consequence of qualifying taxable temporary differences.;;Deferred tax asset: A recognised benefit from qualifying deductible differences or other eligible items, subject to recognition criteria.;;Current tax payable: Tax already associated with current taxable income and payment obligations.;;Permanent difference: A difference that does not reverse in the way assumed for temporary differences.|An asset's accounting carrying amount is ₹80 lakh and its tax base ₹60. Assume this creates a taxable temporary difference under the applicable rules and the relevant future tax rate is 25%.~~Temporary difference = ₹80 − ₹60 = ₹20.;;Simplified deferred tax liability = ₹20 × 25% = ₹5.;;As the difference reverses, the related deferred balance changes, subject to the governing measurement rules.~~The recognised liability is ₹5 under the stated assumptions; recording it is not itself a ₹5 current cash payment.~~Future cash consequences depend on recovery, tax treatment and reversals. A tax asset cannot be treated as cash merely because it appears on the balance sheet.|Reconcile carrying amounts with tax bases.;;Separate temporary, permanent and loss-related items.;;Assess recovery, reversal timing and applicable tax-rate evidence.
T06.13|Trade payables arise from supplier purchases that have not yet been paid. Accrued expenses represent recognised obligations for goods or services received where invoicing or payment may follow later. Both help explain why cash paid differs from expense or inventory additions.~~Supplier funding can reduce immediate cash needs, but overdue balances, lost discounts and fragile supplier relationships can make extended payment terms costly. A financing arrangement may require different classification and disclosures from ordinary trade credit; do not assume every balance in payables is harmless operating funding.|Trade payable: Amount owed to suppliers for invoiced purchases or equivalent ordinary trade obligations.;;Accrued expense: An obligation recognised for received services or other incurred costs before payment.;;Supplier financing: Funding arrangements whose economic and reporting treatment requires separate assessment.;;Overdue balance: An unpaid obligation beyond agreed terms, raising liquidity and relationship concerns.|Opening supplier payables are ₹15 lakh. Credit purchases during the year are ₹60 and supplier payments ₹50. Assume no returns, reclassifications or currency effects.~~Closing payables = ₹15 + ₹60 − ₹50 = ₹25.;;Payables increase ₹10, meaning ₹10 of this year's purchases remains unpaid relative to the opening balance.;;Compare settlement timing with contractual terms and supplier concentration.~~The increase preserves ₹10 of cash in the simplified operating bridge, but the obligation still exists.~~Cash supported by delayed payment should not be mistaken for permanent earnings or cost savings.|Reconcile purchases, accrued costs and cash settlements.;;Review agreed versus actual payment timing.;;Distinguish ordinary trade credit from debt-like financing.
T06.14|A contract asset arises when an eligible recognised right to consideration remains conditional on something other than passage of time. A receivable generally represents an unconditional right subject only to payment timing. A contract liability arises when consideration is received or due before the related performance is completed, subject to applicable requirements.~~These distinctions connect contract performance, billing and collection. Deferred revenue usually describes the liability side; it is not automatically profit waiting to be released without future work or cost. Strong advances can fund operations, while unbilled performance can increase cash needs.|Contract asset: A conditional recognised right arising from performance before an unconditional billing right exists.;;Trade receivable: An unconditional payment claim subject to the relevant recognition assessment.;;Contract liability or deferred revenue: An obligation for promised goods or services after consideration is received or due.;;Billing milestone: A contractual event that may change classification without creating cash.|Assume a service provider has recognised ₹10 lakh of eligible performance, but cannot invoice until a further contractual milestone. It then reaches the milestone and finally receives payment.~~Before the milestone, recognise the qualifying ₹10 contract asset under the stated facts.;;At unconditional billing, transfer the ₹10 to receivables without recognising the same revenue again.;;At collection, increase cash and reduce receivables ₹10.~~Performance, billing and cash collection occur at three different stages.~~Contract terms determine the correct classification. A generic rule that all unbilled amounts are receivables can hide remaining performance conditions.|Read performance and billing conditions.;;Reconcile contract assets, receivables and advances.;;Model future delivery cost and refund risk for deferred revenue.
T06.15|Borrowings are financing obligations governed by principal, interest, maturity, security and covenant terms. Current versus non-current presentation reflects relevant settlement rights and timing rather than the original marketing name of the loan. Cash received from debt increases an asset and a liability; it is not sales revenue.~~The carrying amount can differ from face principal because of fees, discounts and applicable effective-interest measurement. For liquidity analysis, use actual repayment schedules, undrawn facility terms and covenant conditions. For valuation, match debt treatment to the cash flows and claims already included.|Term loan: Borrowing with specified maturity or instalments.;;Revolver or overdraft: A facility with drawing and repayment mechanics under its agreed terms.;;Secured versus unsecured debt: A distinction affecting contractual claims and potential recovery.;;Fixed versus floating rate: A distinction in interest exposure rather than maturity.|A business borrows ₹100 lakh, repays ₹20 principal during the year and borrows another ₹30. Assume no fee adjustments. Next-year scheduled principal is ₹25.~~Closing principal = ₹100 − ₹20 + ₹30 = ₹110.;;Net borrowing = ₹30 − ₹20 = ₹10.;;Separate ₹25 due next year from the remaining ₹85 under the simplified maturity assumptions.~~Debt rises ₹10 even though gross borrowing is ₹30.~~FCFE uses net borrowing because both new financing and repayment affect cash available to equity. Debt proceeds do not increase operating revenue.|Reconcile opening debt, new draws and repayments.;;Read rates, collateral, maturity and covenants.;;Stress debt service and refinancing access rather than only the closing balance.
T06.16|A lease liability measures recognised obligations for eligible lease payments discounted using the applicable rate. Its subsequent movement generally includes interest accrual, payments and relevant remeasurement or modification effects. It must be analysed separately from the right-of-use asset's depreciation schedule.~~A fixed lease commitment creates continuing financing-like cash requirements even where EBITDA excludes the related depreciation and interest. EV bridges and cash-flow forecasts need consistent lease treatment: subtracting a lease liability while also discounting cash flows after full equivalent lease payments can double count the claim.|Current lease liability: The eligible near-term portion under relevant classification rules.;;Non-current lease liability: Remaining recognised obligations outside current classification.;;Remeasurement: Adjustment for relevant changes in lease assumptions or terms.;;Excluded variable payments: Payments whose initial-liability treatment differs under applicable rules and still require cash analysis.|Opening lease liability is ₹100 lakh, effective annual interest is 8%, and the year-end payment is ₹25. Assume no changes to terms.~~Interest accrual = ₹8.;;Principal reduction within the payment = ₹25 − ₹8 = ₹17.;;Closing liability = ₹100 + ₹8 − ₹25 = ₹83.~~A ₹25 cash payment reduces principal by only ₹17 because it also services interest.~~The right-of-use asset can have a different closing amount. Liability, expense and cash each follow their own reconciliation.|Inspect payment and discount-rate assumptions.;;Reconcile interest, principal, modifications and maturity.;;Ensure lease obligations and operating valuation cash flows are treated consistently.
T06.17|A provision is an obligation with uncertain timing or amount recognised when the governing conditions are met. It differs from an ordinary payable with a relatively settled amount and timing. Pension and employee-benefit obligations can involve long periods, demographic assumptions, discounting and plan assets.~~An estimate does not mean the obligation is imaginary. Recognition can reduce current profit before payment, while later settlement uses cash and reduces the balance. Pension funded status compares eligible obligations and plan assets, but contribution requirements and accounting expense can differ materially.|Provision: A recognised obligation satisfying the relevant recognition criteria despite uncertainty.;;Contingent obligation: An item whose disclosure or recognition depends on the applicable uncertainty criteria.;;Defined-contribution plan: An arrangement in which the employer's obligation follows qualifying contribution terms.;;Defined-benefit plan: An arrangement promising benefits and requiring actuarial liability assessment.|A company sells products with warranties and recognises a ₹6 lakh estimated qualifying obligation. During the next period it pays ₹2 in claims and revises remaining expected claims to ₹5. Ignore discounting.~~Initial provision and expense are ₹6.;;Payments reduce the balance to ₹4.;;The revised remaining estimate requires a ₹1 increase to reach ₹5.~~Total claims cash paid is ₹2; the provision closes at ₹5 after the estimate update.~~Neither the initial charge nor the later estimate increase alone pays cash. Compare estimation changes with actual claims and management incentives.|Identify the obligation and recognition basis.;;Review assumptions, discount rates and historical settlement.;;Separate expense, funding, plan assets and actual cash payments.
T06.18|Share capital reflects the legally and accounting-defined capital issued; securities premium records eligible consideration above the relevant nominal amount. Retained earnings accumulate relevant profits less distributions and other required adjustments. These are components of book equity rather than separate bags of cash.~~Equity can rise through retained profit without new share issuance, and cash can be spent even while retained earnings remain high. Market capitalisation values current ownership claims using market prices; it is not simply share capital plus accumulated earnings.|Share capital: Recorded issued capital under the relevant legal and accounting structure.;;Securities premium: Eligible excess consideration above nominal issued capital.;;Retained earnings: Accumulated relevant earnings and distributions.;;Book equity versus market value: Accounting residual and investor pricing answer different questions.|A company issues 10 lakh shares with ₹1 nominal value for ₹5 each, then earns ₹8 lakh and pays ₹3 dividends. Assume no costs or other movements.~~Cash proceeds = 10 lakh × ₹5 = ₹50 lakh.;;Share capital = ₹10 lakh and premium = ₹40 lakh.;;Retained earnings increase ₹8 − ₹3 = ₹5 lakh.~~Total book equity rises ₹55 lakh under the simplified assumptions.~~The ₹5 retained-earnings increase is an accounting accumulation; the company may have invested the related resources in inventory or equipment.|Reconcile issued capital, premium and retained earnings.;;Separate owner transactions from operating performance.;;Compare book value with market or intrinsic equity using appropriate methods.
T06.19|Reserves classify particular equity movements or appropriations; their meaning depends on the specific reserve and governing rules. Treasury shares represent the entity's own shares held under arrangements requiring the applicable equity treatment. Non-controlling interests represent outside ownership in controlled subsidiaries within consolidated accounts.~~These categories change the allocation and presentation of claims, not automatically operating cash. A reserve labelled available does not guarantee distributable cash or legal dividend capacity. Treasury-share purchases use cash and can reduce outstanding shares, but do not create operating revenue.|Revaluation or OCI reserve: Equity arising from particular measurement changes under governing rules.;;Statutory or other reserve: A category with specified legal, contractual or accounting meaning.;;Treasury shares: Own shares whose recognition and deduction treatment depend on the relevant framework.;;Non-controlling interests: Outside owners' interests in consolidated subsidiaries.|An illustrative company has book equity ₹100 lakh and buys back its own shares for ₹10 cash. Assume the shares remain held as treasury shares and the relevant treatment deducts them from equity.~~Cash falls ₹10.;;Record the ₹10 equity deduction under the applicable treasury-share method.;;Book equity becomes ₹90, with no operating profit created by the purchase.~~The buyback redistributes capital and ownership claims; it is not an operating expense or operating revenue in this simplified case.~~Per-share measures can improve through a smaller denominator even when business earnings stay constant. Consider purchase price, funding and alternatives.|Identify each reserve's origin and restrictions.;;Trace own-share transactions and outstanding-share counts.;;Match NCI ownership with consolidated operating and equity measures.
T06.20|Contingent liabilities and commitments describe potential or future obligations that may not be recognised as ordinary liabilities under current accounting criteria. Guarantees expose an entity to another party's failure; capital commitments describe agreed future spending; legal claims can involve uncertain outcomes. Exact recognition and disclosure depend on the relevant standard and facts.~~Absence from the main balance-sheet totals does not imply absence of economic risk. Credit and valuation analysis should examine maximum exposure, likelihood, timing, collateral and links among obligations. A guarantee can become most costly when the guarantor's own business is also under stress.|Contingent liability: A potential or otherwise qualifying unrecognised obligation under applicable disclosure rules.;;Financial guarantee: A contract whose recognition and risk assessment require its governing instrument rules.;;Capital commitment: Agreed future acquisition or construction spending.;;Operating purchase commitment: Future contractual buying requirements with relevant economic and reporting consequences.|A parent guarantees ₹50 lakh of affiliate borrowing. Assume the affiliate can pay in the base case but would leave ₹20 unpaid in a specified downturn; the parent also has ₹30 contracted capex next year.~~Record the guarantee's terms, recognising it as required by the applicable rules rather than automatically treating all guarantees identically.;;Model a ₹20 guarantee cash call in the downside scenario.;;Add the separate ₹30 capex requirement to the funding forecast without assuming it is current debt.~~The downside funding need includes ₹50 of these specified future cash uses, even though their accounting presentation can differ.~~Scenario exposure is not automatically the accounting recognised amount. Distinguish risk analysis from recognition and avoid counting the same underlying debt twice.|Read commitments, guarantees and litigation notes.;;Assess probability, timing, correlations and enforceability.;;Include credible cash calls in stress testing and disclose measurement uncertainty.
